A malformed reboot message crashed pid 1, and crashed it again on the way down.

sys_reboot's format string asks for two conversions and only ONE argument was passed:

    __simple_printf("... with opt %d and cmd '%s' - terminating\n", cmd);

so %d consumed the pointer and %s then read whatever happened to be in the next varargs slot and
walked it as a string. __simple_vsnprintf guards NULL for %s but nothing guards garbage, so this
faulted every single time a process asked to reboot.

AND launchd IS ONE OF THEM, WHICH MADE IT RECURSIVE. launchd is pid 1, so its own fatal-signal
handler ends in reboot(0). The fault inside sys_reboot therefore re-entered sys_reboot through
sigexc_handler, and each pass added six frames until the stack ran out. The core shows the cycle
plainly, the same six addresses repeating:

    __simple_vsnprintf <- faults
    __simple_printf
    sys_reboot
    __darling_bsd_syscall
    handler_linux_to_bsd
    sigexc_handler
    sig_restorer
    __simple_printf   <- and round again

That was reported as a SIGSEGV in the guest /sbin/launchd, which is true and useless: a stack
overflow, not a bad pointer, and nothing to do with the application that was running.

MEASURED AFTER: no core at all, and the message finally says what it always meant to say,

    ALERT: The process has asked for system reboot with opt 0 and cmd '(null)' - terminating

A scan of the 187 countable __simple_printf/kern_printf calls in the emulation layer, the guest
frameworks and libxpc finds no other format that disagrees with its argument list.

--- a/darling/src/libsystem_kernel/emulation/src/xnu_syscall/bsd/impl/misc/reboot.c
+++ b/darling/src/libsystem_kernel/emulation/src/xnu_syscall/bsd/impl/misc/reboot.c
@@ -10,7 +10,15 @@
 
 long sys_reboot(int opt, const char* cmd)
 {
-	__simple_printf("ALERT: The process has asked for system reboot with opt %d and cmd '%s' - terminating\n", cmd);
+	/*
+	 * BOTH ARGUMENTS. The format asks for two and only `cmd` was passed, so `%d` consumed the
+	 * pointer and `%s` then read whatever happened to be in the next varargs slot and walked it.
+	 * `%s` guards NULL but nothing guards garbage, so this faulted every time a process asked to
+	 * reboot -- and launchd is one of them, and launchd is pid 1, so ITS crash handler asks to
+	 * reboot too: the fault re-entered here through sigexc_handler and recursed until the stack
+	 * ran out. A six-frame cycle in the core is what named it.
+	 */
+	__simple_printf("ALERT: The process has asked for system reboot with opt %d and cmd '%s' - terminating\n", opt, cmd);
 	sys_exit(1);
 	return -ENOTSUP;
 }
